{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1249"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_1461584496#0_2060658110","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1249","rank":2,"snippet":", \"segment\": \"Archduke Franz Ferdinand - The Man Whose Death Triggered World War 1 - World History Online You are here: World History Online > Modern History > Archduke Franz Ferdinand - The Man Whose Death Triggered World War 1 Archduke Franz Ferdinand - The Man Whose Death Triggered World War 1 The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand and the Austro-Hungarian heir to the throne triggered <cover>World War 1 but did not cause it. In the summer of 1914, Europe only needed an incident to ignite the inevitable war and his death was exactly the incident that set of the chain of events resulting in 15 to 20 million deaths. Table of Contents The Heir Apparent Franz Ferdinand's Character and Political Views Marriage to Sophie Chotek Visit to Sarajevo, Assassination and Aftermath Quick Facts</cover>Archduke Franz Ferdinand was the nephew of Austro-Hungarian Emperor Francis Joseph I and was the third in line for the Imperial throne. He became heir apparent after the suicide of his cousin, the Crowned Prince Rudolph in 1889 although he became officially the first in line for the Imperial throne after his father's death in 1896. Lack of charisma, quick temper and radical political "}}
