{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1256"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1546096330#1_3246122602","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1256","rank":4,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1546096330#1_3246122602\" , \"end_char\": 1315.000000 , \"headings\": \"Battle of the Somme Facts Battle of the Somme Facts 20 Battle of the Somme Facts for Kids Pictures of the Battle of the Somme \" , \"segment\": \"United Kingdom and rench Empire Total <cover>Casualties: 1,000,000+ 20 Battle of the Somme Facts for Kids The Battle of the Somme was a military campaign on the Western Front during World War 1. </cover>The Battle of the Somme was fought between the German Empire, the United Kingdom and the French Third Republic. The Battle of the Somme was fought on both sides of the upper reaches of the River Somme in France. The Battle of the Somme was fought between July 1st, 1916 and November 18th, 1916. The Battle of the Somme was fought for 4 months, 2 weeks and 3 days. The Battle of the Somme was fought because the British Empire wanted to aid the French soldiers at Verdun and to weaken the German Army, hopefully hastening a victory for the Entente Powers. "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"28/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1546096330#1_3246122602","relevance":2,"reason":"This is another student-facing Battle of the Somme facts segment giving 1,000,000+ casualties for a major WWI battle. It can illustrate the scale of losses, but it is not dedicated to the overall number of deaths in World War I.","learned":"The Facts Just for Kids Somme page repeats the 1,000,000+ total casualties figure for that WWI battle."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ ww1 \"world war i\" \"first world war\") (+ casualties deaths killed) (+ lesson students classroom teaching) (+ \"million\" total))","reason":"Search for less restrictive classroom/lesson resources that include total WWI death or casualty figures in millions, likely to yield direct teaching evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ ww1 \"world war i\" \"first world war\") (+ casualties deaths killed) (+ lesson students classroom teaching) (+ \"million\" total))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_488404486#4_976052396","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1257","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_488404486#4_976052396\" , \"end_char\": 3449.000000 , \"headings\": \"Germany to Blame for Ww1 Germany to Blame for <cover>Ww1 You May Also Find These Documents Helpful Popular Essays Join millions of other students and start your research Become a StudyMode Member \" , \"segment\": \"Around 9.7 million military personnel and 6.8 million civilians were killed, </cover>not to mention those who went missing or were never found. A question that still lingers, even one hundred years later, is what caused this? Which circumstances could have led to a war so large and so deadly? In truth, there was no single cause for the outbreak of the First World War. The causes are much more complex than those of the Second World War and include short, intermediate and long term factors that all culminated to cause the July Days in 1914. These factors include militarism, nationalism, imperialism, the alliance system, and industrialization. In the following paragraphs, I will explore the factors listed above and allow you to come to your own conclusion as to "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"29/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_488404486#4_976052396","relevance":2,"reason":"This StudyMode student-essay passage directly gives WWI death figures of about 9.7 million military personnel and 6.8 million civilians, but it is a user-generated essay about causes/blame rather than a dedicated, trustworthy teaching or student resource on understanding WWI deaths.","learned":"Some essay pages state WWI deaths as roughly 9.7 million military personnel and 6.8 million civilians, but source quality is weak."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"world war 1\" \"world war i\" ww1) (+ \"how many people died\" \"death toll\" \"were killed\") (+ \"for kids\" students children))","reason":"Search directly for student-facing pages that answer how many people died in World War I, which should yield more grade-3 evidence with clear death-toll figures."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"world war 1\" \"world war i\" ww1) (+ \"how many people died\" \"death toll\" \"were killed\") (+ \"for kids\" students children))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1546088573#1_3246103763","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1258","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1546088573#1_3246103763\" , \"end_char\": 1168.000000 , \"headings\": \"World War 1 Facts World War 1 Facts 20 World War 1 Facts for Kids \" , \"segment\": \"The Entente Powers Military Death Toll: 9,900,000+ fatalities Civilian <cover>Death Toll: 7,700,000+ fatalities 20 World War 1 Facts for Kids</cover>World War 1 was fought between July 28th, 1914, and November 11th, 1918. This war is also called the Great War, the Seminal Catastrophe or simply the First World War. World War 1 is often abbreviated as WW1 or WWI. World War 1 was fought between two alliances, the Entente Powers and the Central Powers. The Entente Powers included the British Empire, Empire of Japan, French Republic, Kingdom of Italy, Russian Empire and the United States of America. The Central Powers include Germany, Austria-Hungary, the Ottoman Empire and Bulgaria. The Entente Powers were victorious and won World War 1. However, both alliances had millions of military and civilian deaths, suffered from diseases and major economic hardship.\" , \"start_char\""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"30/50"}}
