{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1258"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_418935115#5_454942749","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1258","rank":4,"snippet":"1_doc_17_418935115#5_454942749\" , \"end_char\": 5102.000000 , \"headings\": \"Effect of World War I on children in the United States Effect of World War I on children in the United States Contents Background of World War I Impact on daily life Home life Technology Impact on Education Post-war Youth organizations Boy Scouts Camp Fire Girl Aftermath See also References Sources and further reading Books for elementary school <cover>children \" , \"segment\": \"Home life Girls too young for paid jobs learned how they could help the war effort. Over 21 million people were killed or injured in World War I; </cover>in many cases, all of the men in one family were killed, numerous cities in Europe were destroyed, and family life throughout the world was greatly affected. While the United States suffered the fewest casualties, family life was still greatly affected as husbands were deployed and wives had to begin working. As the war draft suddenly removed many men from factory work, the factories began soliciting for women workers in traditionally male-dominated areas. Therefore, women had less time to spend at ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1546088573#3_3246106198","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_1428484762#7_2349425476","rank":3,"relevance":1}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"31/50"}}
